introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How does the world stay connected?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on <strong>Interconnectivity</strong> and <strong>Standardisation</strong>. You will explore how heterogeneous systems communicate through common rules (<strong>Protocols</strong>) and the physical architecture required to move data globally, from local LANs to the massive WAN of the Internet."
  journey: "You'll begin by defining network types (LAN, WAN, VPN) and hardware (Routers, Switches). Then you'll map out <strong>Topologies</strong> and model data flow using <strong>Packet Switching</strong> and <strong>TCP/IP</strong>. Finally, you'll secure these networks using <strong>Encryption</strong> and <strong>Firewalls</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Network Fundamentals"
    icon: "fa-network-wired"
    content: |
      <p>Networks are defined by scale and architecture:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>LAN (Local):</strong> Small area, high speed (e.g., Home/School).</li>
          <li><strong>WAN (Wide):</strong> Large area, connects LANs (e.g., The Internet).</li>
          <li><strong>VPN (Virtual Private):</strong> A secure tunnel over a public WAN.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Packet Switching"
    icon: "fa-envelope-open-text"
    content: |
      <p>Data doesn't travel in one big chunk. It is broken into <strong>Packets</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Breakdown:</strong> File sliced into packets.</li>
          <li><strong>Routing:</strong> Each packet takes the fastest route (independent paths).</li>
          <li><strong>Reassembly:</strong> Packets reordered at the destination.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Protocols (The Rules)"
    icon: "fa-handshake"
    content: |
      <p>Computers must agree on how to talk. We use standard protocols:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>TCP/IP:</strong> The fundamental language of the internet.</li>
          <li><strong>HTTP/HTTPS:</strong> Web pages (Secure vs plain).</li>
          <li><strong>DHCP:</strong> Assigning IP addresses automatically.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Security & Encryption"
    icon: "fa-lock"
    content: |
      <p>The internet is public. To keep secrets, we use math.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Firewall:</strong> A filter that checks incoming/outgoing packets.</li>
          <li><strong>Symmetric Encryption:</strong> Same key to lock and unlock (Fast, but how to share key?).</li>
          <li><strong>Asymmetric Encryption:</strong> Public key to lock, Private key to unlock (Secure key exchange).</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Protocol"
    definition: "A set of rules defining how two computers communicate (e.g., format, speed, error checking)."
  - term: "Bandwidth"
    definition: "The theoretical maximum amount of data that can be transmitted over a connection in a given time."
  - term: "Latency"
    definition: "The time delay between the request for data and the receipt of the data."
  - term: "Packet Switching"
    definition: "Method of slicing data into packets, routing them individually, and reassembling them at the destination."
  - term: "Router"
    definition: "A device that forwards data packets between computer networks (typically joining a LAN to a WAN)."
  - term: "Switch"
    definition: "A device that connects devices within a LAN and uses MAC addresses to filter traffic."
  - term: "Encryption"
    definition: "Encoding data so that only authorized parties can understand it."
  - term: "VPN"
    definition: "Virtual Private Network. Extends a private network across a public network allowing secure data exchange."
  - term: "Topology"
    definition: "The physical or logical arrangement of a network (e.g., Star, Mesh, Bus)."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Web vs Internet"
    correction: "Thinking they are the same. The Internet is the infrastructure (Roads). The Web is just one service on it (Traffic)."
  - mistake: "WiFi = Internet"
    correction: "Assuming a WiFi signal means you have Internet. You can have strong WiFi (LAN) but no connection to the ISP (WAN)."
  - mistake: "Switch vs Router"
    correction: "Thinking they do the same job. Switches create a network (LAN). Routers connect networks (LAN to WAN)."

